<h1>Remote Type Fidelity</h1>
<p>A flow passes values from one step to the next as if every step ran in the same process: a step puts a value on
	its result, and the flow hands it to the next step's config. When the steps run on endpoints, those values have
	to cross the network, and a naive transport would flatten them on the way: a tuple would come back as a list,
	an enum member as its string, a nested structure as a bag of generic containers. Code that checks
	<code>isinstance</code>, uses a tuple as a dict key, or compares enum members would then behave differently on a
	remote step than on a local one.</p>
<p>Orcraft keeps the types intact. Arguments are serialized to the endpoint and results serialized back with
	their Python types preserved, lists, tuples and dicts are walked so nested structures keep their shape, and enum
	members and instances of the suite's own classes arrive as what they were, because the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/packages-uploading/">test package</a> is on the endpoint too. This is
	what lets a flow be written against <code>localhost</code> and moved to a lab later without touching the code
	that wires one step's output into the next step's input. The framework reference covers it under
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/steps/#type-fidelity">type fidelity</a>.</p>
<p>The <strong>Type Fidelity</strong> test of the <strong>Endpoints</strong> group makes the guarantee visible: one
	step assembles typed values on an endpoint, and a second step on the same endpoint checks every type after the
	round trip through the controller.</p>

<h2 id="producing-typed-values">Producing typed values</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_type_fidelity/") }}
<p>The first step solves a maze and builds values of the kinds that a careless transport would damage: a tuple of
	dimensions, an enum member from the suite's own <code>MazeSolveStatus</code>, tuple coordinates, and a dict
	that nests tuples, an enum and another dict:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_step(self) -> OrcraftTestStepResult:
	self.result = StepProduceMazeTypedDataResult()

	exe_folder = normalize_long_path(self.config.maze_executable_folder)
	width = int(self.config.width)
	height = int(self.config.height)

	result = MazeApp(exe_folder, "solve", width=width, height=height).run()

	# Determine solve status from the run result
	status = result.status
	maze_area = width * height

	# Assemble typed data that exercises RPC type preservation:
	# - tuple (would flatten to list without proper serialization)
	# - enum (would flatten to string value without proper serialization)
	# - nested dict with mixed types
	self.result.dimensions = (width, height)
	self.result.solve_status = status
	self.result.start_coords = (0, 0)
	self.result.metadata = {
		"bounds": (0, 0, width - 1, height - 1),
		"tags": ("maze", "tutorial", "generated"),
		"status": status,
		"nested": {
			"area": maze_area,
			"pair": (True, False),
		},
	}

	Logger.log(f"dimensions   = {self.result.dimensions!r}", color=Logger.Color.BLUE, module_tag=False)
	Logger.log(f"solve_status = {self.result.solve_status!r}", color=Logger.Color.BLUE, module_tag=False)
	Logger.log(f"start_coords = {self.result.start_coords!r}", color=Logger.Color.BLUE, module_tag=False)
	Logger.log(f"metadata     = {self.result.metadata!r}", color=Logger.Color.BLUE, module_tag=False)

	return self.result') }}
<p>The values are plain attributes of the result rather than declared result fields. Structured data is rarely
	something a person wants as a column in the controller, and plain attributes travel to the flow without ever
	becoming one; the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/#plain-attributes">framework reference</a> draws the
	line between the two.</p>

<h2 id="verifying-the-types">Verifying the types</h2>
<p>The flow passes the four values to the second step as config values, exactly as it would pass a path or a
	number:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('step_2_result = self.run_step(
	StepVerifyMazeTypedData,
	endpoint=self.config.test_endpoint,
	dimensions=step_1_result.dimensions,
	solve_status=step_1_result.solve_status,
	start_coords=step_1_result.start_coords,
	metadata=step_1_result.metadata,
)') }}
<p>The second step declares them as plain attributes of its config, prints each one with its type, and asserts the
	type with a <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/checkpoints/">checkpoint</a>:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('class StepVerifyMazeTypedDataConfig(OrcraftTestStepConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Verify Maze Typed Data")
	dimensions = None
	solve_status = None
	start_coords = None
	metadata = None


...

	self.checkpoint(isinstance(dimensions, tuple), "\'dimensions\' is tuple", f"\'dimensions\' type is {type(dimensions).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(dimensions, tuple) and len(dimensions) == 2, "\'dimensions\' has length 2", "\'dimensions\' length is not 2")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(solve_status, MazeSolveStatus), "\'solve_status\' is MazeSolveStatus enum", f"\'solve_status\' type is {type(solve_status).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(solve_status, MazeSolveStatus) and solve_status in list(MazeSolveStatus), "\'solve_status\' has valid value", "\'solve_status\' value is not valid")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(start_coords, tuple), "\'start_coords\' is tuple", f"\'start_coords\' type is {type(start_coords).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(metadata, dict), "\'metadata\' is dict", f"\'metadata\' type is {type(metadata).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(bounds, tuple), "\'metadata.bounds\' is tuple", f"\'metadata.bounds\' type is {type(bounds).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(tags, tuple), "\'metadata.tags\' is tuple", f"\'metadata.tags\' type is {type(tags).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(meta_status, MazeSolveStatus), "\'metadata.status\' is MazeSolveStatus enum", f"\'metadata.status\' type is {type(meta_status).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(nested, dict), "\'metadata.nested\' is dict", f"\'metadata.nested\' type is {type(nested).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(pair, tuple), "\'metadata.nested.pair\' is tuple", f"\'metadata.nested.pair\' type is {type(pair).__name__}")
	self.checkpoint(isinstance(area, int), "\'metadata.nested.area\' is int", f"\'metadata.nested.area\' type is {type(area).__name__}")') }}
<p>Each value made two trips: from the first step on the endpoint to the flow on the controller, and from the flow
	back to the second step on the endpoint. A type lost on either leg would fail its checkpoint.</p>

<h2 id="running-the-test">Running the test</h2>
<p>Select the <strong>Type Fidelity</strong> test, set its <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>, and choose the
	macOS machine as the <strong>Test Endpoint</strong> so the values cross both a network and an operating
	system boundary. Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, then
	<strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm. When the run finishes, search the log for
	<code>'metadata.nested.area'</code>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/remote-type-fidelity/typed-values-in-log.png", "Log of the Type Fidelity run on GMG-TEST-5 showing the values printed by the verify step with their types, tuple for dimensions, MazeSolveStatus for solve_status, dict for metadata and tuple for its nested pair, followed by a passing checkpoint for every type", "large") }}
<p>The verify step prints what it received: <code>(5, 8)</code> with type <code>tuple</code>,
	<code>&lt;MazeSolveStatus.SOLVED: 'solved'&gt;</code> with type <code>MazeSolveStatus</code>, the nested
	<code>(True, False)</code> still a <code>tuple</code>, and the area still an <code>int</code>. Every
	checkpoint below passes.</p>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/remote-type-fidelity/type-fidelity-details.png", "Type Fidelity Details view with the Produce Maze Typed Data and Verify Maze Typed Data steps both on GMG-TEST-5 and passing", "large") }}
<p>Both steps ran on <strong>GMG-TEST-5</strong>, and the typed values never appear in the tree: they were plain
	attributes, handed from step to flow to step, and only the checkpoints that judged them left a trace. Choose
	<strong>localhost</strong> as the endpoint instead and the same test passes the same way, which is the point:
	the code that passes values between steps does not know or care where the steps run.</p>

<p>The endpoints have so far always been there. The next page,
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/controlled-endpoints-disconnection/">Controlled Endpoints
	Disconnection</a>, takes one away on purpose and waits for it to come back.</p>
